WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.013
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEIC preparation.

2
00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:09.095
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:17.898
Unit fifteen: Part 6: Text Completion. Today you will complete emails, notices and articles using grammar and context.

4
00:00:18.198 --> 00:00:20.665
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.030
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:27.186
Text completion.

7
00:00:28.183 --> 00:00:31.448
Text completion means filling gaps in a short text.

8
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.073
Part 6 is text completion.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:37.533
Your turn. Say: Text completion.

10
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:41.867
Text completion.

11
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:43.815
Context.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:47.072
Context means the words around a gap.

13
00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:49.532
Use context to choose.

14
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:53.094
Your turn. Say: Context.

15
00:00:55.933 --> 00:00:56.900
Context.

16
00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.802
Linking word.

17
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:02.722
Linking word means a word that connects ideas.

18
00:01:03.317 --> 00:01:04.829
However is a linking word.

19
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:08.150
Your turn. Say: Linking word.

20
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.140
Linking word.

21
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:13.873
Insert.

22
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:17.754
Insert means to put a sentence in the right place.

23
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.955
Insert the best sentence.

24
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.866
Your turn. Say: Insert.

25
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:26.597
Insert.

26
00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.339
Logical.

27
00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.431
Logical means making sense.

28
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:33.761
The sentence is logical.

29
00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.793
Your turn. Say: Logical.

30
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:40.751
Logical.

31
00:01:41.517 --> 00:01:42.669
Refer to.

32
00:01:43.683 --> 00:01:45.439
Refer to means to mention.

33
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.166
This refers to the earlier point.

34
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.542
Your turn. Say: Refer to.

35
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.297
Refer to.

36
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:56.865
Pronoun.

37
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.012
Pronoun means a word like it or they.

38
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:02.663
The pronoun refers to the manager.

39
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.751
Your turn. Say: Pronoun.

40
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.636
Pronoun.

41
00:02:10.383 --> 00:02:11.350
Cohesion.

42
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.675
Cohesion means how sentences connect.

43
00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:16.946
Good cohesion helps you.

44
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.435
Your turn. Say: Cohesion.

45
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.464
Cohesion.

46
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:28.534
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:31.406
Visal, what is Part 6?

48
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:41.624
You read four short texts, such as emails or notices, each with several gaps. You choose a word, a phrase or a whole sentence that fits.

49
00:02:42.717 --> 00:02:44.739
How is it different from Part 5?

50
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:56.766
Some gaps need you to read the sentence before or after, because the answer may be a linking word like however or therefore, or a verb tense that matches the whole text.

51
00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.780
How do I choose a missing sentence?

52
00:03:00.883 --> 00:03:11.384
Read the sentences before and after the gap. The best choice connects to both: it may refer back with a pronoun and lead into the next idea.

53
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:14.080
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:14.917 --> 00:03:18.205
Grammar focus: Strategy: read around the gap.

55
00:03:18.505 --> 00:03:19.622
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:20.317 --> 00:03:24.583
The system will be down tomorrow. Therefore, please save your work today.

57
00:03:25.483 --> 00:03:29.097
The best sentence connects to both the line before and the line after.

58
00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:36.073
Read the whole text quickly, then choose the answer that fits the grammar and connects the ideas before and after the gap.

59
00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:40.084
Be careful! Don't say: I read only the gap.

60
00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:44.428
Say: I read around the gap.

61
00:03:44.728 --> 00:03:46.042
Context matters.

62
00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:49.776
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:51.974
A Notice Completed.

64
00:03:52.274 --> 00:03:55.887
A notice tells staff that the system will be offline on Saturday.

65
00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.246
One gap needs a linking word before please save your work.

66
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:05.160
The first part gives a problem, so the next part gives an action.

67
00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:08.166
Therefore is a logical connector.

68
00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:12.153
Dara chooses Therefore and rereads the whole notice.

69
00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:17.292
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.405
Part 6 has blank short texts.

71
00:04:20.705 --> 00:04:23.615
A, four. B, one. C, ten.

72
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:28.980
The answer is A: four.

73
00:04:30.117 --> 00:04:31.698
A gap may need a blank.

74
00:04:31.998 --> 00:04:35.837
A, picture. B, whole sentence. C, map.

75
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.829
The answer is B: whole sentence.

76
00:04:42.983 --> 00:04:44.449
Therefore shows blank.

77
00:04:44.749 --> 00:04:48.681
A, a contrast. B, a place. C, a result.

78
00:04:52.233 --> 00:04:54.372
The answer is C: a result.

79
00:04:55.517 --> 00:04:56.982
What is the notice about?

80
00:04:57.282 --> 00:05:01.969
A, the system being offline. B, a party. C, a trip.

81
00:05:05.533 --> 00:05:08.067
The answer is A: the system being offline.

82
00:05:09.217 --> 00:05:11.181
To choose a sentence, read blank.

83
00:05:11.481 --> 00:05:15.889
A, nothing. B, before and after the gap. C, only the title.

84
00:05:19.433 --> 00:05:22.153
The answer is B: before and after the gap.

85
00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:26.472
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:27.317 --> 00:05:29.409
How do you answer Part 6 questions?

87
00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:39.671
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:39.971 --> 00:05:53.759
I read the whole text quickly to understand the idea. For each gap I read the sentences before and after it. I choose the answer that fits the grammar and connects logically, and then I read the sentence again.

89
00:05:54.059 --> 00:05:55.734
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:01.624
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: read around the gap.

91
00:06:01.924 --> 00:06:06.690
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

92
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.561
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

93
00:06:09.861 --> 00:06:13.662
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

